2026-06-06 09:30:13,695 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] ### Investigation Question Responses
2026-06-06 09:30:13,695 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] **Q1: What processes were running?**
2026-06-06 09:30:13,696 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] ~80 processes captured in memory. Normal system services, McAfee AV suite (masvc, mfefire, mcshield, HipMgmt, shstat), Sysmon64.exe, NCPA monitoring (ncpa_listener, ncpa_passive), F-Response (subject_srv.exe). User mhill had an active RDP session (Session 5) with explorer.exe, Outlook, Chrome, GROOVE.EXE, and MSOSYNC.EXE. **Malicious processes:** WmiPrvSE.exe (PID 2676), three parallel PowerShell chains, 5+ short-lived rundll32.exe instances, and one persistent rundll32.exe (PID 7100).
2026-06-06 09:30:13,697 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] **Q2: Are there hidden processes?**
2026-06-06 09:30:13,697 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] Could not definitively compare psscan vs pslist for wkstn-05 specifically, as the pslist data was not separately indexed for this system. No obvious hidden processes identified from psscan alone.
2026-06-06 09:30:13,698 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] **Q3: What network connections exist?**
2026-06-06 09:30:13,698 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] - 6 connections to C2 proxy 172.16.4.10:8080 (2 CLOSED, 4 CLOSE_WAIT, no PIDs)
2026-06-06 09:30:13,699 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] - 2 WinRM connections to 172.16.5.21:5985 (lateral movement)
2026-06-06 09:30:13,700 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] - 1 inbound SMB from base-rd-02 (172.16.6.11:445)
2026-06-06 09:30:13,701 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] - 2 HTTPS connections to 172.16.4.6:443
2026-06-06 09:30:13,701 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] - F-Response on port 3262 to examiner 172.16.5.50
2026-06-06 09:30:13,702 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] - RDP listening on 3389
2026-06-06 09:30:13,702 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] **Q4: Is there evidence of code injection?**
2026-06-06 09:30:13,702 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] **Yes.** Five short-lived rundll32.exe processes (2-21 second lifetimes) spawned by PowerShell PID 1332 are characteristic of Metasploit process injection. One persistent rundll32.exe (PID 7100, 337 handles, no exit time) represents a long-running injected implant. Malfind data was not separately indexed for this system's memory dump.
2026-06-06 09:30:13,703 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] **Q5: What commands were executed?**
2026-06-06 09:30:13,704 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] Cmdline data for wkstn-05-specific processes was not available in the indexed sources. The attack chain structure (WMI→PowerShell 64-bit→PowerShell 32-bit) implies encoded PowerShell commands were used for the initial stager, consistent with the pattern seen on other systems.
2026-06-06 09:30:13,704 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] **Q6: Is there evidence of lateral movement?**
2026-06-06 09:30:13,705 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] **Yes.** Two directions:
2026-06-06 09:30:13,705 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] - **Inbound:** WMI remote code execution (WmiPrvSE.exe Session 0, indicating remote origin); SMB from base-rd-02
2026-06-06 09:30:13,706 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] - **Outbound:** WinRM to 172.16.5.21:5985 (PowerShell Remoting)
2026-06-06 09:30:13,706 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] **Q7: Is there evidence of privilege escalation?**
2026-06-06 09:30:13,707 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] The WMI→PowerShell chain executes in Session 0 (SYSTEM context), indicating the attacker already had elevated privileges when they initiated the WMI execution. This implies compromised domain credentials with admin rights on this workstation.
2026-06-06 09:30:13,708 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] **Q8: What persistence mechanisms exist?**
2026-06-06 09:30:13,708 INFO mulder.orchestrator.display: [dashboard] Registry hive data was not available for wkstn-05, so persistence mechanisms could not be verified through registry analysis. The persistent rundll32.exe (PID 7100) was running continuously but this is memory-resident, not disk-persistent.
